# The Raspberry Pi 5's boot, in two stages as the demo's: this starts
# boot as pid 1, then the drivers and the file server; boot starts the
# rest, /etc/system. It has booted on a board, to the shell, the
# compositor and the Ethernet link (README, The Raspberry Pi 5).
#
# uart finds and drives the debug UART, the 3-pin header between the HDMI
# ports, and serves console. xhci rp1 drives RP1's two USB controllers,
# which the firmware leaves set up (config.txt's pciex4_reset=0, which
# l0 card writes), and one usb-hid serves every USB keyboard as keyboard
# and every mouse as pointer. hvs drives HDMI0 through the HVS and serves display;
# wm waits for the display it serves. It has shown the desktop; to look
# at the HVS a stage at a time, start /sbin/hvs probe, list or iommu,
# each of which exits once done. With no argument it serves.
# It reads the HVS only if the firmware brought a monitor up on HDMI0 at
# boot, since otherwise the HVS is unclocked. hdmi-audio waits for the
# display, then plays sound on HDMI0 through dma40's channel 6 and serves
# audio, which play uses: play /etc/chime.wav. sdhci pi5 serves the
# microSD card in the slot, if there is one, as a disk; one fs ram serves
# a volume in memory, /Volumes/tmp, registered as tmpfs, and mounts the
# card's volumes, a FAT32 boot partition among them, registered as fs.
# With no card it serves tmp alone.
#
# rtc clock serves clock, the time, from the RTC in the Pi's power
# controller, which it asks the firmware for through the mailbox; it
# keeps time over a power cut only with a battery on the RTC connector.
#
# eeprom, run at the shell, asks the firmware the same way for the
# bootloader's configuration, and writes the update that changes it.
#
# gem drives the Ethernet port, RP1's GEM and the Broadcom PHY behind
# it, with the board's address from the firmware, and serves nic; inet
# listens to it and serves inet at the address a DHCP server gives it,
# as on QEMU. There is no random here, no virtio-rng, so TCP's first
# sequence numbers are keyed with no secret.
#
# The demo's other drivers have nothing to drive here: virtio-*, pcie
# virt and sdhci pci.
start /sbin/boot
start /sbin/uart
start /sbin/rtc clock
start /sbin/xhci rp1
start /sbin/usb-hid
start /sbin/hvs
start /sbin/hdmi-audio
start /sbin/sdhci pi5
start /sbin/fs ram
start /sbin/gem
start /sbin/inet dhcp

# Drivers: these may map devices and take their interrupts.
dev /sbin/uart
dev /sbin/rtc
dev /sbin/eeprom
dev /sbin/xhci
dev /sbin/hvs
dev /sbin/hdmi-audio
dev /sbin/gem
dev /sbin/sdhci

# Arguments when the shell's run gives none, wherever / is.
args /bin/ping 3
args /bin/nap 500
